1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,381
A pressure cooker whistles
when the rice is done.

2
00:00:03,381 --> 00:00:05,616
A kettle sings when the water boils.

3
00:00:06,016 --> 00:00:09,575
Each is a signal that says,
stop now, it is finished.

4
00:00:10,025 --> 00:00:12,302
Diya's mother washes
vegetables in light pink water.

5
00:00:12,302 --> 00:00:14,266
A few minutes later, the pink has faded.

6
00:00:14,616 --> 00:00:16,256
The pink colour got used up.

7
00:00:16,256 --> 00:00:17,716
It reacted with something.

8
00:00:18,216 --> 00:00:20,809
Chemists use that same pink as a signal.

9
00:00:20,809 --> 00:00:24,237
By the end, you will know
how one drop says, stop.

10
00:00:24,887 --> 00:00:26,029
Here is the job.

11
00:00:26,029 --> 00:00:29,507
We have a solution, but we
do not know how strong it is.

12
00:00:29,857 --> 00:00:33,512
We add a known solution,
drop by drop, from a burette.

13
00:00:33,512 --> 00:00:36,937
A burette is a long glass
tube with marks and a tap.

14
00:00:37,337 --> 00:00:38,878
This is a titration.

15
00:00:38,878 --> 00:00:43,243
When the two have reacted exactly,
we stop and read the volume.

16
00:00:43,243 --> 00:00:47,121
In a redox titration, that
reaction is a redox reaction.

17
00:00:47,721 --> 00:00:50,345
Let's run one on our molecule bench.

18
00:00:50,345 --> 00:00:52,838
Purple permanganate in the burette.

19
00:00:52,838 --> 00:00:55,797
Iron two plus in the flask, with acid.

20
00:00:56,147 --> 00:00:59,556
Each drop of permanganate
loses its colour at once.

21
00:00:59,556 --> 00:01:03,344
Iron two plus uses it up,
and becomes iron three plus.

22
00:01:03,744 --> 00:01:08,174
Manganese goes from plus seven to
plus two, so it takes five electrons.

23
00:01:08,174 --> 00:01:09,584
Each iron gives one.

24
00:01:09,984 --> 00:01:14,549
When the last iron is used up, the
next drop has nothing to react with.

25
00:01:14,949 --> 00:01:15,894
So it stays.

26
00:01:15,894 --> 00:01:17,637
The flask turns light pink.

27
00:01:17,637 --> 00:01:19,150
That is the end point.

28
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:22,092
So permanganate is its own signal.

29
00:01:22,092 --> 00:01:24,130
We call it a self indicator.

30
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:27,942
An indicator is a colour signal
that shows the end point.

31
00:01:28,342 --> 00:01:30,531
Potassium dichromate is different.

32
00:01:30,531 --> 00:01:34,009
Orange dichromate turns into
green chromium three plus.

33
00:01:34,009 --> 00:01:36,698
That change is hard to see at the end.

34
00:01:37,098 --> 00:01:39,972
So we add an indicator
called diphenylamine.

35
00:01:39,972 --> 00:01:43,889
Just after the end point, it
gives an intense blue colour.

36
00:01:44,389 --> 00:01:46,109
A third way uses iodine.

37
00:01:46,109 --> 00:01:48,685
Copper two plus and iodide give iodine.

38
00:01:48,685 --> 00:01:51,958
Then thiosulphate turns the
iodine back into iodide.

39
00:01:52,358 --> 00:01:54,272
Starch is the signal here.

40
00:01:54,272 --> 00:01:56,776
Starch and iodine make a deep blue.

41
00:01:56,776 --> 00:02:00,501
When the last iodine is used
up, the blue disappears.

42
00:02:01,101 --> 00:02:03,221
Now, how do we use the volume?

43
00:02:03,221 --> 00:02:04,519
With the n factor.

44
00:02:04,869 --> 00:02:08,979
The n factor is the number of electrons
that one formula unit gives or

45
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:09,879
takes.

46
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:11,799
Permanganate in acid, five.

47
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:14,772
Dichromate, six, because
it has two chromium.

48
00:02:14,772 --> 00:02:16,240
Iron two plus, one.

49
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:17,545
Thiosulphate, one.

50
00:02:17,945 --> 00:02:21,406
At the end point, electrons
given equal electrons taken.

51
00:02:21,406 --> 00:02:25,269
So molarity times n factor times
volume is equal on both sides.

52
00:02:25,669 --> 00:02:28,522
Molarity times n factor
is called normality.

53
00:02:28,522 --> 00:02:31,057
So N one V one equals N two V two.

54
00:02:31,057 --> 00:02:33,204
This is the law of equivalents.

55
00:02:33,854 --> 00:02:36,383
Now compare two signals side by side.

56
00:02:36,383 --> 00:02:38,941
With permanganate, no extra indicator.

57
00:02:38,941 --> 00:02:41,881
The purple fades, then a faint pink stays.

58
00:02:42,281 --> 00:02:44,221
With iodine, starch is added.

59
00:02:44,221 --> 00:02:46,827
The blue stays, then the blue disappears.

60
00:02:47,327 --> 00:02:48,552
Pause and think.

61
00:02:48,552 --> 00:02:51,871
Why does a permanganate
titration need no indicator?

62
00:02:55,071 --> 00:02:59,966
Because permanganate is deep purple,
and its product is almost colourless.

63
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:01,785
One extra drop shows up.

64
00:03:02,435 --> 00:03:03,335
Remember this.

65
00:03:03,302 --> 00:03:06,293
At the end point, electrons
given equal electrons taken.

66
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:08,874
Now, one worked example.

67
00:03:08,874 --> 00:03:10,755
These are example figures.

68
00:03:11,055 --> 00:03:15,069
Twenty millilitres of point zero two
molar permanganate react with twenty

69
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:17,859
five millilitres of an
iron two plus solution.

70
00:03:17,859 --> 00:03:19,084
Find its molarity.

71
00:03:19,584 --> 00:03:20,900
Permanganate side.

72
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:23,927
Point zero two, times five, times twenty.

73
00:03:23,927 --> 00:03:24,994
That is two.

74
00:03:25,744 --> 00:03:26,644
Iron side.

75
00:03:26,639 --> 00:03:29,478
Molarity, times one, times twenty five.

76
00:03:30,228 --> 00:03:32,932
So molarity times twenty five equals two.

77
00:03:32,932 --> 00:03:35,179
The molarity is point zero eight.

78
00:03:35,929 --> 00:03:37,470
Check with the equation.

79
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:40,324
One permanganate reacts
with five iron two plus.

80
00:03:40,324 --> 00:03:43,962
Point four millimoles of
permanganate, two millimoles of iron.

81
00:03:43,962 --> 00:03:44,862
Same answer.

82
00:03:45,497 --> 00:03:46,958
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:03:47,308 --> 00:03:49,255
Students forget the n factor.

84
00:03:49,255 --> 00:03:52,604
They write molarity times
volume equal on both sides.

85
00:03:53,004 --> 00:03:55,978
That works only when both n factors match.

86
00:03:55,978 --> 00:03:58,795
Multiply each side by its own n factor.

87
00:03:59,445 --> 00:04:00,722
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:00,722 --> 00:04:03,317
Boards ask about indicators
and the end point.

89
00:04:03,317 --> 00:04:06,845
Entrance tests give volumes and
ask for a molarity or a volume.

90
00:04:07,445 --> 00:04:08,552
Exam level now.

91
00:04:08,552 --> 00:04:12,827
Point one molar dichromate, in acid,
oxidises thirty millilitres of point

92
00:04:12,827 --> 00:04:14,468
two molar iron two plus.

93
00:04:14,468 --> 00:04:16,727
What volume of dichromate is needed?

94
00:04:16,727 --> 00:04:17,880
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:24,367
The answer is A.

96
00:04:24,367 --> 00:04:25,353
Ten millilitres.

97
00:04:25,853 --> 00:04:26,759
Iron side.

98
00:04:26,759 --> 00:04:29,425
Point two, times one, times thirty.

99
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:30,498
That is six.

100
00:04:30,998 --> 00:04:32,276
Dichromate side.

101
00:04:32,276 --> 00:04:34,798
Point one, times six, times V.

102
00:04:34,798 --> 00:04:36,884
So point six V equals six.

103
00:04:37,384 --> 00:04:38,802
V is ten millilitres.

104
00:04:38,802 --> 00:04:40,753
B forgot the n factor of six.

105
00:04:41,353 --> 00:04:42,893
Here is your recap.

106
00:04:42,893 --> 00:04:44,476
Screenshot this card.

107
00:04:47,676 --> 00:04:49,862
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

108
00:04:50,262 --> 00:04:51,162
One.

109
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,562
Why is permanganate
called a self indicator?

110
00:04:54,362 --> 00:04:55,262
Two.

111
00:04:54,873 --> 00:04:58,939
In an iodine titration with
starch, what shows the end point?

112
00:04:59,739 --> 00:05:00,639
Three.

113
00:05:00,291 --> 00:05:02,949
What is the n factor
of dichromate in acid?

114
00:05:03,849 --> 00:05:06,533
Next time: how can a redox
reaction light a bulb?

115
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:09,327
We find out in redox reactions
and electrode processes.
